Git is a distributed version control system that tracks changes in your source code over time, allowing you to collaborate, roll back mistakes, and integrate with CI/CD pipelines seamlessly.

The Three Stages of Git

Git manages your work across three main states:

Working Directory: The local folder where you create and edit your files.

Staging Area (Index): A checkpoint where you mark modified files to be included in your next snapshot.

Repository (.git directory): The permanent history where Git stores your committed snapshots.

Essential Git Workflow Commands

Initialize a repository: git init

Turns an existing local project folder into a Git repository.

Check repository status: git status

Shows which files have been modified, untracked, or staged.

Stage files for commit:

git add index.html       # Stages a specific file
git add .                # Stages all modified files in the current folder

Save a snapshot (Commit): git commit -m "Add homepage header layout"

Records staged changes permanently with a descriptive message.

View history : git log --oneline

Displays a simplified list of past commits.

Branching and Collaboration

Branching allows you to develop new features or fix bugs in isolation without touching the stable main codebase.

Create and switch to a new branch:

git switch -c feature/login
# Alternatively: git checkout -b feature/login

Merge changes back to main:

git switch main
git merge feature/login

Sync with a remote server (e.g., GitHub):

git clone <repo-url>: Downloads an entire remote repository to your local machine.

git push origin feature/login: Uploads your local branch commits to GitHub.

git pull origin main: Fetches and integrates the latest changes from the remote server into your current branch.

Quick Check

If you edit an existing file called app.py, which command must you run first before you can save it into your permanent history with git commit?

git add app.py moves the file from your Working Directory to the Staging Area, preparing it for your next snapshot.

Next Step: Committing and Pushing

Once staged, complete the cycle with these two commands:

Commit the staged change locally:

git commit -m "Update logic in app.py"

Push the commit to your remote repository (e.g., GitHub):

git push origin main


Why This Matters for CI/CD

In modern DevOps pipelines:

Every time you run git push, your remote repository (like GitHub or GitLab) detects the new commit.

That push automatically triggers automated CI/CD runners to build your code, run unit tests, and verify that nothing broke.


what is branching and pull requests (PRs) and how branching and pull requests (PRs) work? 

In modern DevOps and CI/CD pipelines, you never write code directly on the main (production) branch. Instead, every new feature or bug fix follows an isolated Branching -->Pull Request (PR) --> Merge workflow.

1. The 6-Step Branch & PR Workflow

Step 1: Update your local main branch
Always start with the freshest code from the remote server:

git switch main
git pull origin main

Step 2: Create and switch to a feature branch
Keep branches focused on one task and name them clearly (e.g., feature/login-page or fix/auth-bug):

git switch -c feature/add-readme

Step 3: Make changes, stage, and commit
Make your edits, stage the files, and record a clear commit message:

git add README.md
git commit -m "Add project setup instructions to README"

Step 4: Push the feature branch to the remote repository
Upload your branch to GitHub or GitLab:

git push -u origin feature/add-readme
(The -u or --set-upstream flag tells Git to link this local branch to the remote branch so you can simply run git push or git pull later).

Step 5: Open a Pull Request (PR)

Go to your repository on GitHub/GitLab.

Click Compare & pull request.

Set the base branch to main and the compare branch to feature/add-readme.

Add a clear title and description explaining what you changed and why.

Step 6: Automated CI checks & Code Review

As soon as the PR is opened, the automated CI pipeline runs builds and tests against your branch.

Teammates review your code, leave feedback, or approve the changes.

Once tests pass and approvals are granted, the PR is merged into main.


2. Handling Merge Conflicts

A merge conflict occurs when two branches modify the exact same lines in a file, and Git doesn't know which version to keep.

When you run git merge or merge a PR, Git flags conflicting files with conflict markers:

<<<<<<< HEAD (Current branch / main)
print("Hello, World!")
=======
print("Hello, DevOps Engineer!")
>>>>>>> feature/add-readme

How to resolve it:

Open the conflicting file in your editor.

Choose the code you want to keep and delete the conflict markers (<<<<<<<, =======, >>>>>>>).

Stage and commit the resolved file:

git add .
git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict in app.py"
git push origin feature/add-readme


Quick Practice Scenario

Imagine you are working on a team. A teammate merged new code into main while you were working on your branch feature/user-auth.

Before opening your Pull Request to avoid conflicts, what is the best sequence of commands to update your local branch with the latest changes from main?

Step 1: Fetch and update your local main

git switch main
git pull origin main

Step 2: Switch back to your feature branch

git switch feature/user-auth

Step 3: Merge the updated main into your feature branch

git merge main

Step 4: Resolve any conflicts, commit, and push

# Resolve any conflicts in your editor if they appear
git add .
git commit -m "Merge latest main into feature/user-auth"
git push origin feature/user-auth

Why do it this way?

It ensures your local main branch stays up to date with the remote repository.

You test and resolve any merge conflicts safely on your own branch before opening or updating your Pull Request, ensuring the automated CI pipeline runs cleanly on GitHub/GitLab.

1. Ignoring Unwanted Files (.gitignore)

You never want to commit build artifacts, temporary files, environment variables, or dependencies (like node_modules/ or .env files with secret keys).

Create a file named .gitignore in your repository root.

Add patterns to exclude files and folders:

Code snippet
# Ignore sensitive environment variable files
.env

# Ignore dependencies and package directories
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Ignore OS and build output files
.DS_Store
dist/
build/


2. Stashing Changes Temporarily (git stash)

If you are in the middle of editing files and need to switch branches urgently without committing unfinished work, use the stash:

Save uncommitted work to the stash: git stash

Restore your stashed changes later: git stash pop

List all stashed items: git stash list

3. Undoing Mistakes Safely

Discard changes in a local file (before staging):

git restore app.py
Unstage a file (keep the changes in your working directory):

git restore --staged app.py
Amend the last commit message (before pushing):

git commit --amend -m "Updated commit message"
Safely undo a pushed commit by creating an inverse commit:

git revert <commit-hash>
(In DevOps, git revert is preferred over destructive commands like git reset --hard because it preserves shared history).

4. Checking Differences (git diff)

View unstaged changes: git diff

View staged changes (ready to be committed): git diff --staged

Compare two branches:

git diff main feature/user-auth

Quick Practice Scenario

You modified config.py and realized your changes broke everything. You have not run git add yet. Which command restores config.py back to its last committed state?

git restore config.py discards all uncommitted local modifications in config.py and returns it to the exact state of your last commit.


Practice Challenge

Suppose you ran git add . and accidentally staged your secret .env file containing API keys, but you have not run git commit yet.

Which command will safely unstage .env without deleting the file from your computer?

git restore --staged .env (or git restore --staged <filename>) removes the file from the Staging Area while keeping all your edits safely intact in your working directory.

